\begin_body

\begin_layout Section
Open System Dynamics using the Lindblad Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One way to treat dissipation in a quantum system is to use the Lindblad equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\hat{\rho}}{dt}=-\frac{i}{\hbar}\left[\hat{H},\hat{\rho}\right]+\sum_{j}\hat{L}_{j}\hat{\rho}\hat{L}_{j}^{\dagger}-\frac{1}{2}\left\{ \hat{L}_{j}^{\dagger}\hat{L}_{j},\hat{\rho}\right\} ,\label{eq:Lindblad}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \hat{L}_{j}\right\} $
\end_inset

 are called Lindblad operators or dissipation channels
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
As before,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 is the Hamiltonian and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\rho}$
\end_inset

 is the density operator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This is a matrix differential equation,
 like the quantum Liouville equation [see Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:QuantumLiouville"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Lindblad equation contains a term called the Liouvillian,
 which is the right-hand side of Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:QuantumLiouville"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This part is said to be the unitary part or the Liouvillian part
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is responsible for the unitary (closed-system) dynamics of a quantum state
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Lindblad"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) also has an additional term,
 called the Lindbladian or the dissipator:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}=\sum_{j}\hat{L}_{j}\hat{\rho}\hat{L}_{j}^{\dagger}-\frac{1}{2}\left\{ \hat{L}_{j}^{\dagger}\hat{L}_{j},\hat{\rho}\right\} \label{eq:Dissipator}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The Lindbladian is responsible for the non-unitary dynamics of a model
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The effects of the environment on the system are contained within the Lindblad operators
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A specific choice of the Lindblad operators is a specific model for the environment
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
Lindblad Operators for the Quantum Harmonic Oscillator - Dissipation (Relaxation)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For the quantum harmonic oscillator,
 we can use a single Lindblad operator,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\{ \hat{L}_{1}\right\} =\left\{ \frac{1}{\sqrt{T_{1}}}\hat{a}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This causes an 
\series bold
exponential decay in system energy
\series default
 with time constant 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset

 is a measure of system-environment coupling:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A short 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset

 allows a fast exponential decay,
 meaning the environment and our quantum system are strongly coupled
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Energy decay means that the system can lose energy
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 the system is said to be an open system
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The loss of energy is also called 
\series bold
relaxation
\series default
,
 since the system relaxes from a high-energy state toward the ground state
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The loss of energy is also called energy 
\series bold
dissipation
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
After applying this operator for a long time,
 we should expect to find the system in its ground state
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The code below calculates the time evolution of a QHO under relaxation,
 and results are shown in Figures 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:qho-dynamics-2-1"

\end_inset

 (position basis) and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:qho-dynamics-3-1"

\end_inset

 (energy basis)
\end_layout

